<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Della Rovere</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of della Rovere</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The della Rovere were a family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> that rose through the Church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Francesco della Rovere</a> became Pope Sixtus IV (1471–1484) and his nephew Giuliano became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> (1503–1513). Through adoption and papal backing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-maria-i-della-rovere">Francesco Maria I della Rovere</a> became Duke of Urbino in 1508, and the family ruled Urbino until the death of Francesco Maria II in 1631.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/della-rovere.jpg" alt="Della Rovere" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZuppaDiCarlo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stemma_della_famiglia_Della_Rovere.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Della Rovere means of the oak tree. The family had humble origins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a>, and gained power and influence through nepotism and ambitious marriages arranged by its two popes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Francesco della Rovere</a> was born in 1414 into a poor family, the son of Leonardo della Rovere of Savona. He became a Franciscan, rose to Minister General of his order and then to cardinal, and had a reputation for unworldliness until his election as pope in 1471. As Sixtus IV he claimed descent from a noble Della Rovere family, the counts of Vinovo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piemonte</a>, and adopted their coat of arms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sixtus IV at once set about giving power and wealth to his nephews of the Della Rovere and Riario families. Within months of his election he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Giuliano della Rovere</a>, the future Julius II, and Pietro Riario both cardinals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and four other nephews also received the red hat. He made Giovanni della Rovere, who was not a priest, prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and arranged for him to marry into the da Montefeltro family, the dukes of Urbino. Sixtus built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a>, which is named after him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guidobaldo-da-montefeltro">Guidobaldo da Montefeltro</a>, who had no children, adopted his sister's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-maria-i-della-rovere">Francesco Maria I della Rovere</a>, a nephew of Julius II. Through the intercession of Julius, Guidobaldo named him heir to the Duchy of Urbino in 1504, and Francesco Maria inherited it in 1508, beginning the line of Della Rovere dukes. Julius II, pope from 1503, was a patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> and other artists and began the demolition and rebuilding of St Peter's Basilica. The Basilica of San Pietro in Vincoli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was the family church. Members of the family were also dukes of Sora and lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sena-gallica">Senigallia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Della Rovere dukes of Urbino were Francesco Maria I (1490 to 1538), Guidobaldo II (1514 to 1574) and Francesco Maria II (1549 to 1631). In the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a> the duchy was incorporated into the papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>. The last descendant of the family was Vittoria della Rovere, the only child of Federico Ubaldo, who lived from 1622 to 1694. She married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-ii-de-medici">Ferdinando II de' Medici</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>, and their children were Cosimo III, the longest-reigning monarch of Tuscany, and Francesco Maria de' Medici, a prince of the Church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Della Rovere” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
